## This notebook is presented in response to phd thesis reviewer comments, superivsor comments and student's response. The objectives of research is:

The dissertation will be organized around a single, vertically integrated three-layer architecture connected by one explicit end-to-end data chain with physical units:

• Layer 1 (Demand and Renewable Forecasting — combining Study 3's comparative forecasters (https://doi.org/10.1002/bte2.70079) and Study 4's Transformer forecaster(https://doi.org/10.1002/bte2.20250009)): Takes historical demand, calendar features, and weather data to predict commercial building loads (in kW) and available solar/wind generation (in MW). We will define an explicit mathematical aggregation and scaling equation mapping kW-level building load forecasts onto distribution feeders (IEEE 33-bus, 3.72 MW peak load) and bulk transmission load buses (IEEE 14-, 57-, and 118-bus in MW/MVar), and state this aggregation clearly as a modeling assumption.

• Layer 2 (Forecast-Driven Storage Scheduling — Study 1(https://doi.org/10.1049/tje2.70126)): Uses Layer 1 forecasts to compute battery charging and discharging active/reactive set-points (MW/MVar) and update State-of-Charge (SoC ∈ [10%, 90%]) outside the power-flow solver under a Rainflow cycle-degradation model.

• Layer 3 (Physics-Guided Graph Security Screening and AC-OPF Execution — combining Study 2's(https://doi.org/10.1155/je/8588270) graph stability models and Study 4's optimal power flow dispatch): Passes the candidate bus injections (P, Q, V set-points) and network topology into the physics-guided graph model (PI-GAT / TGCN) to screen branch Fast Voltage Stability Index (FVSI < 0.80 pass/fail) prior to executing the verified schedule in a closed-loop pandapower AC-OPF (runopp) environment. A dedicated table and flowchart in Section 3.6 will map the exact inputs, outputs, units, and scale transitions across all three layers.

